<file path="docs/ENVIRONMENT.md">
  1: # Scholar-Flow Environment Setup Guide
  2: 
  3: This guide explains all environment variables for both backend (Express + Prisma) and frontend (Next.js + NextAuth), where to get them, and how to configure them for development and production.
  4: 
  5: ## Locations and Quick Start
  6: 
  7: - Backend env file: `apps/backend/.env`
  8: - Frontend env file: `apps/frontend/.env.local`
  9: 
 10: Recommended bootstrap (from repo root):
 11: 
 12: ```bash
 13: # Copy example envs
 14: cp apps/backend/.env.example apps/backend/.env
 15: cp apps/frontend/.env.example apps/frontend/.env.local
 16: ```
 17: 
 18: Then fill in the variables below.
 19: 
 20: ## Generate Strong Secrets
 21: 
 22: - NEXTAUTH_SECRET: a 32+ byte random string
 23: 
 24: ```bash
 25: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
 26: ```
 27: 
 28: - JWT/other secrets: you can reuse the same method.
 29: 
 30: ## Backend (.env)
 31: 
 32: Required unless marked optional.
 33: 
 34: - DATABASE_URL
 35:   - What: PostgreSQL connection string
 36:   - Dev example: `postgresql://username:password@localhost:5432/scholar_flow`
 37:   - How: Create a DB named `scholar_flow` locally. For pgvector, ensure extension is installed: `CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;`
 38: 
 39: - NODE_ENV
 40:   - What: Node environment (development|production)
 41:   - Dev: `development`
 42: 
 43: - PORT
 44:   - What: Backend port
 45:   - Dev: `5000`
 46: 
 47: - FRONTEND_URL
 48: - USE_PGVECTOR
 49:   - What: Feature flag to enable vector operations (embeddings & similarity)
 50:   - Dev: `false` by default; set `true` after pgvector extension and embeddings are ready
 51: 
 52:   - What: CORS allowlist origin
 53:   - Dev: `http://localhost:3000`
 54: 
 55: - NEXTAUTH_SECRET
 56:   - What: Secret used by Auth.js JWT; also used by backend middleware for token verification
 57:   - How: Generate via Node crypto (see above) and keep in sync with frontend NEXTAUTH_SECRET
 58: 
 59: - JWT_SECRET
 60:   - What: JWT signing secret for backend-only tokens (if used)
 61:   - How: Generate via Node crypto (see above)
 62: 
 63: - EXPIRES_IN, REFRESH_TOKEN_SECRET, REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN
 64:   - What: Access/refresh token lifetimes and secret (if using refresh flow)
 65:   - Dev example: `EXPIRES_IN=1d`, `REFRESH_TOKEN_SECRET=<random>`, `REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN=7d`
 66: 
 67: - RESET_PASS_TOKEN, RESET_PASS_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN, RESET_PASS_LINK
 68:   - What: Password reset token secret, expiry, and frontend link to reset page
 69:   - Dev example: `RESET_PASS_TOKEN=<random>`, `RESET_PASS_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN=5m`, `RESET_PASS_LINK=http://localhost:3000/reset-password`
 70: 
 71: - Email/SMTP (choose one approach)
 72:   - EMAIL, APP_PASS (Gmail App Password)
 73:     - What: Sender email and its app password
 74:     - How: Enable 2FA in Google Account > Security > App passwords > create one for “Mail”
 75:   - OR SMTP\_\* (hosted SMTP)
 76:     - SMTP_HOST, SMTP_PORT, SMTP_USER, SMTP_PASSWORD
 77:     - How: Use your provider’s credentials (e.g., SendGrid, Mailgun)
 78: 
 79: - AI Providers & Feature Flags
 80:   - OPENAI_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY, DEEPSEAK_API_KEY
 81:     - How: Generate from respective provider consoles (OpenAI, Google AI Studio, DeepSeek). Leave blank to disable that provider.
 82:   - AI_PROVIDER_FALLBACK_ORDER
 83:     - What: Comma-separated priority list (e.g., `openai,gemini,deepseek`). The orchestrator uses the first configured provider.
 84:   - AI_FEATURES_ENABLED
 85:     - What: Master switch for backend AI features. Set to `false` to bypass all AI enrichments.
